<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?>
<FinalDraft DocumentType="Script" Template="No" Version="2">
  <Content>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RIDGE ROAD - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Wind comes down off the ridge in long flat gusts, pushing the grass one way and then, for no reason, the other.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A road climbs the ridge in switchbacks. Nobody has driven it since the washout, and the grass is taking it back.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>MARA, 40s, walks it with a pack on and four days in her legs. She has the face of someone who fixes things and is never thanked.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>DELACROIX, 50s, walks behind her with a paper map folded to the right square. He does not look at it. He looks at the road, and counts.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Thirty-one.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They take the bend. Gravel lets go under Mara’s boot and goes quietly over the edge.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You don’t have to do that out loud.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The map says forty. I’d like to know when it’s lying.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Cloud sits on the ridge above them like a lid somebody forgot to close.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How long has he been up there?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Three weeks. On his own.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They said two.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They say a lot of things.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The road ends at the washout, a bite taken out of the mountain. Mara stops where the road stops, then goes round it on the rock.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX (CONT'D)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Thirty-eight.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. FIELD STATION - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>MARA and DELACROIX come up the last of the slope, packs on, bent into the wind. Below them the road they walked is a thin grey line, going all the way down.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The station: a prefab hut bolted to the rock, a radio mast guyed four ways, a fuel drum, a generator crouched under a tarp. Everything that can rattle, rattles.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The hut door opens before they reach it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>TEO, 20s, comes out in two jumpers and no coat. Thin. He has the look of someone who has rehearsed what to say to people and forgotten all of it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You came.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We said we would.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>People say that on the radio. It’s different, seeing it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He shakes Delacroix’s hand, then Mara’s, then Delacroix’s again.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You’re Teo.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I’m Teo. Sorry. Come in, it’s warmer. It isn’t, but it’s out of the wind.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The wind leans on all three of them. The mast answers it with a low note, like someone humming through their teeth.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO (CONT'D)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That’s the mast. You get used to it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do you?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Teo walks them round the station at a pace that makes it hard to see any of it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO (CONT'D)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Fuel. Water. Rain gauge. It’s been fine the whole time.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He points up at the mast without looking at it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO (CONT'D)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It sings in a north wind. One long note. In a west wind it’s two, and they don’t agree.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And an east wind?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We don’t get an east wind.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He thinks about it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO (CONT'D)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We got one once.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mara crouches by the generator and lifts the tarp. Oil on the housing, old and new. One bolt is missing. Another turns in her fingers with no thread left to hold it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She looks at it for a long moment. Then she lets the tarp fall.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Well?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It runs.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>That is all she says. Delacroix has worked with her long enough to count what she leaves out.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I kept it running. Every day. I wrote it down.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I believe you.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Teo nods too many times and goes inside. The wind shuts the door behind him, hard.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. FIELD STATION - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>One room. A cot, a table, one chair. A tarp lashed over a gap in the north wall where a panel used to be. It breathes with the weather.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the wall above the radio, a map of the valley. Four pins in it, running down the line to the lowest station.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into the handset)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Base, this is Field Station. We’re up.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Static. Then a voice, patient, a long way off.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Field Station, Iversen. Good to hear you. How’s the boy?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Delacroix glances at Teo, who is shelving their tins in order of size.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tidy.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Pickup is tomorrow, first light. Relief team brings the new keeper up and the three of you down.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>First light.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Weather permitting. It always permits, until it doesn’t.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He sets the handset down. Mara is looking at the map.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Why four pins?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>One for every station on the line. We’re the top one.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He touches the lowest pin, then takes his finger away as if it were warm.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RADIO MAST - DUSK</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The light goes early up here. What is left of it is the colour of weak tea.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>MARA climbs the mast, harness clipped to the ladder, a spanner through her belt. The wind pulls at her like it wants her for something.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>DELACROIX stands at the foot, the safety line paid out through his gloves.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How many rungs?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I don’t count them.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Forty-four. I counted for you.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She reaches the antenna. It hangs off true, its bracket worked loose by a winter of north wind.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX (CONT'D)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Can I ask you something?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You’re going to.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Why did you take this job? You could fix things anywhere. Somewhere with walls.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A gust takes the mast and leans it an inch. She waits it out, cheek against the cold steel, eyes shut.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Below her, the line goes taut in his hands and stays taut until the gust is gone.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nothing up here pretends to work.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Delacroix thinks about that. Decides not to agree with it out loud.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She sets the spanner to the bracket. Two turns. The antenna comes true, and the mast’s low note changes pitch, very slightly, as if it has noticed.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is that it?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That’s it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She doesn’t come down. She is looking past him, past the hut, out across the valley to the far ridge.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A light. One small steady point of it, low on the far slope, where the map shows nothing at all.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It does not flicker. Nothing out there has any reason to be lit.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Mara? You’re losing the light.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The point goes out, like somebody closing a hand around it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Coming down.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She unclips and starts down, rung by rung. She does not mention it. She will not mention it for some time.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. FIELD STATION - EVENING</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Three of them round a table built for one. Tins on a camping stove. The tarp on the north wall breathes in, out.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>TEO eats fast, like the food might be taken back.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Four pins. Tell me about them.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We call down the line at six every evening. Everybody answers. We all say goodnight.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everybody?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Teo puts his fork down.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The fourth hasn’t answered. Nine days.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A pause. The stove ticks as it cools.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nine days, and you didn’t report it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I did. Every night, after I called them, I reported it to base.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nobody answered that either.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Delacroix looks at Mara. Mara looks at the radio. The radio hisses and says nothing, the way it has said nothing for nine days.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Iversen answered today.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Iversen answered you.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RIDGE - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Wind, and under the wind a cold that means business.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>DELACROIX walks the ridge with a torch, the beam no use against anything further off than his own boots.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He finds the marker cairn where the map says it will be: a chest-high stack of flat stones, sighted down the valley toward the fourth station. In clear weather you could see its lamp from here.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>There is no lamp tonight. There is no clear weather.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He sets the torch on the ground and looks at the cairn properly.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It has been rebuilt. Carefully. Every stone seated, every gap chinked with smaller stones. Nobody did this in a hurry, and nobody did it for the weather.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hello?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The wind takes the word and does not bring it back.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He knows this cairn. He built the last one himself, eleven years ago, and he knows how many stones went into it. Twenty-six.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He counts them. His lips move.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX (CONT'D)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Twenty-five.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He counts again, slower, touching each one with a gloved finger, top to bottom.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX (CONT'D)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Twenty-five.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>One short. Whoever rebuilt it built it well and built it one stone short, and a careful person does not make that mistake by accident.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He sweeps the torch around his feet. Grass. Rock. No stone lying loose.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Down in the valley, where the fourth station should be, there is only dark.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He switches the torch off and stands in it for a moment, to see what his eyes will do.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nothing out there changes. Nothing out there has changed for nine days.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He turns the torch back on and walks back toward the hut, faster than he came.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. FIELD STATION - LATER</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mara is asleep on the cot in her boots. Teo is not asleep, and not pretending to be.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The radio crackles. Delacroix comes in with the cold still on him and goes straight to the handset.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Field Station, Iversen. Field Station, do you read?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Go ahead.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Weather’s closing over the pass. Relief is delayed.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Delayed how long?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Static, for long enough that Teo sits up.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Twenty-three hours, earliest. We’ll hold here until it...</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The hiss closes over the rest.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Iversen. Come back.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nothing. Delacroix sets the handset down gently, as if it might still be listening.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He takes a pencil from his pocket and writes 23 on the wall beside the map, small and neat. He looks at it. Underlines it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Was that him? Was that really him?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Go to sleep, Teo.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. FIELD STATION - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The wind has found a new direction and does not like that one either.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>TEO wheels the quad bike out of the lean-to. Headlamp on. A jerrycan strapped across the back, and a second map, the survey copy, rolled under his arm.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>MARA comes out after him, pulling on her coat.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Teo.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It’s four hours down if the track’s clear. I’ll see them. I’ll come back and tell you.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don’t.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nine days. Somebody has to go and look.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>In the morning. With us.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>In the morning there’ll be a reason to wait until the afternoon.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He is not wrong, and she knows it, and that is the worst part.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Call us when you get there.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I always call.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He kicks the bike over. It catches first time, which feels like a verdict.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>DELACROIX is in the doorway now, the lamp behind him.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Four hours down. Four back. The relief is twenty-three.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then I’ll be back before them.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Teo pulls out onto the track. The headlamp swings across the rock, across the fuel drum, and settles on the way down.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They watch the tail light go. Red, small, smaller. It dips below the lip of the ridge, comes up once more on the next switchback, and then does not come up again.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mara keeps looking at the place where it was.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You could have stopped him.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I could have slowed him down.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Behind them, under its tarp, the generator coughs.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Once. A dry, small sound, like someone clearing their throat before bad news. Then it runs on, even and ordinary, as if nothing happened.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Neither of them says anything. Delacroix looks at his watch and does not say the time.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. LOWER STATION - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A hut like theirs, lower and older, in a fold of the valley where the wind cannot quite reach and so sulks instead.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A headlamp comes down the track and stops. TEO kills the engine. The silence is enormous.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The door of the hut is open. Not blown open. Propped, with a stone.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TEO</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hello? It’s Teo. From the top.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nothing. He goes in.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Inside, the station is neat. The cot is made. A mug stands on the table with a skin on its cold tea.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The radio is on. Its dial glows a soft green, the only light in the room.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Teo leans in to read the frequency. He knows the number. It is theirs.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Beside the radio a notebook lies open. Line after line of the same entry, in a hand that gets smaller down the page. Six o’clock. Six o’clock. Six o’clock.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Someone has been listening. Every night. And never once said a word.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Teo picks up the handset. His thumb finds the key.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He holds it down.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. FIELD STATION - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The generator coughs once and dies. MARA works by the light of her phone, hands steady, and does not look up.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Give it a minute. It always comes back.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And if it doesn’t?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She finds the housing bolt by feel. Turns it a quarter turn.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then we do this in the dark.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The wind takes the tarp on the north wall and holds it open like a door nobody opened.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That’s not an answer.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It’s the one I have.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He crosses to the window. Outside, the ridge is a black line against a slightly less black sky.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nineteen hours until the relief team. I counted.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then stop counting.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The generator catches. Dies again. Catches.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Light comes up amber and unsteady, and the room assembles itself around them: the cot, the radio, the map with its four pins.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA (CONT'D)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There. Told you.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You told me a minute. That was six.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I rounded.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She wipes her hands on her thigh and sits, finally, like the sitting costs her something.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You should sleep.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You should stop saying that.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A long moment. The radio hisses, says nothing, hisses.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>If the relief doesn’t come.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They’ll come.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>If.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then we walk out the way we walked in, and it takes four days instead of one, and you complain the entire time.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I don’t complain.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You’re complaining now.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He almost laughs. It is the first honest sound either of them has made all night.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Get some sleep. I’ll take the radio.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She doesn’t argue, which frightens him more than the dark did.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. FIELD STATION - DAWN</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Grey light. The tarp is down. The map is gone from the wall.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>DELACROIX sits exactly where he sat, the handset still in his fist, asleep with his eyes open the way soldiers learn.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The radio speaks. One word, clipped, in a voice neither of them has heard.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He is awake before he knows he is awake.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Say again.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nothing. The hiss closes over the word like water.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX (CONT'D)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Say again.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He turns the dial one notch, then back, then one notch the other way, hunting the frequency the way you hunt a name you have almost remembered.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA (O.S.)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What was it?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She is in the doorway. She has not slept and does not pretend otherwise.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A voice. One word. I couldn’t hold it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Which word?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He looks at the handset, then at her.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELACROIX</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ours.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Outside, very far off, something that is not the wind moves along the ridge.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>FADE OUT.</Text></Paragraph>
  </Content>
